110:#    DIRECT cells, 4 lanes x widths 1/2/4/8 x both arms -- rowed for the two cells above ONLY.  Lane 0: stores 4k `kbcq' (the 8042 data
111:#      port, one reply queued), loads 4l `rdq' (two replies queued).  Lane 1: both arms 4m `cmos' (CMOS REG C, whose
112:#      read clears it).  Lane 2: stores 4h `iir' / 4i `iirw', loads 4j `iirr' (len 1) and 4n `iirr2' (len 2/4/8) (IIR
113:#      0x3fa with TXRDY armed).  Lane 3: 4o `stub3' ALONE -- the 8042 PC-style stub at 0x63, an emulator-model oracle
114:#      (below).  4o `stub0'/`stub1'/`stub2' carry lanes 0-2 again.
115:#      *** THIS SENTENCE ENDED `as a strengthening, never as the sole carrier' AND IS MEASURED FALSE THREE TIMES --
116:#      the ruling's RB1 breached at the very cells the row set was built to satisfy it at.  WWall 169/185 is SIXTEEN
117:#      STUB ROWS AND NOTHING ELSE (lanes 0,1,2,3: no kbcq, no iir, no cmos, no tailq moves); RWall 167/185 is 16 stub
118:#      + 2 tailr, every direct lane; RD2post 161/185 has NO architectural carrier at lanes 1 or 2.  THE CAUSE IS THE
119:#      CHANNELS, NOT THE ROWS: CMOS REG C (its read zeroes it) and ns16550 IIR (its read clears TXRDY once) are
120:#      ONE-SHOT LATCHES, and A LATCH SEPARATES ZERO ACCESSES FROM AT LEAST ONE -- IT CAN NEVER SEPARATE ONE FROM TWO,
121:#      which is exactly and only what a forward-COUNT class asks.  The stub was sole carrier because the stub is an
