140:#      artifacts/lcaisalane/table7_20260916/LEN8_DETERMINATION.md. ***
141:#
142:#  `store_buf()' issues `memory_rw' at psize in {1024, 256, 64, 16, 4, 1} -- src/core/memory.c:839
143:#  declares `size_t psize = 1024;  /* 1024 256 64 16 4 1 */' and :843-845 is
144:#  `while (len >= psize) cpu->memory_rw(cpu, cpu->mem, addr, ..., psize, MEM_WRITE, ...)'.
145:#  PROM GETENV reaches it with a GUEST-SUPPLIED address: cpu_alpha_palcode.c:144 is
146:#  `store_buf(cpu, a2, s, len)'.  **So len 16/64/256/1024 IS guest-reachable at this handler.**
147:#
148:#  WHY NO ROW COVERS IT, and the reason is structural rather than an oversight: this probe is
149:#  INSTRUCTION-DRIVEN and the widest Alpha store is STQ at 8 bytes.  *No instruction-driven row can
150:#  ever express len > 8.*  A row for this cell must drive the NON-INSTRUCTION path -- a PROM GETENV
151:#  whose a2 lands in the ISA window.
152:#
153:#  MEASURED (KILLLEN8_RUN.out, sentinel KILLLEN8_DONE): the clause-(d) mutant
154:#  `len == 0' -> `len == 0 || len > 8' scores **352/352 -- a TOTAL ESCAPE**, with the run's own
155:#  control confirming `W_len8 binary DIFFERS from base', so the edit was live in the object.
156:#  Graded **L2, a floor breach, fix in-round** by the rule CLOSE6_VERDICT.md:77 set down BEFORE the
157:#  leg ran.
158:#
159:#  *** THE PRODUCT CODE IS CORRECT AT THESE WIDTHS AND MUST NOT BE TOUCHED FOR THIS. ***  At len 16,
160:#  `ofs = (relative_addr & 3) % 16' is just `relative_addr & 3', which is in [0,3] and indexes
161:#  safely.  It is the MUTANT that misbehaves, by declining a legal wide access.  What is owed is a
162:#  ROW.
163:#
164:#  *** DISCHARGED 2026-09-18 -- THE ROW NOW EXISTS, IN ITS OWN FILE: regress/lca_widewrite_probe.py. ***
165:#  It drives the non-instruction path this header names -- a PROM GETENV (CALL_PAL 0x3fffffe) whose
166:#  a2 is the ns16550 SCR -- so store_buf issues ONE 16-byte memory_rw into lca_isa.  Measured, every
167:#  value predicted before any run (artifacts/lcalenzerodrop/widewrite_20260918/PRETABLE.out):
168:#      base 3/3 (SCR <- s[3] = 'D');  `&& len <= 8' 2/3;  `len == 0 || len > 8' 2/3
169:#  Both mutants that swept THIS file's 352 rows clean are killed there.  Kept in a SEPARATE file on
170:#  purpose: folding it in here would move 352 measured rows and owe a full re-measure to show nothing
171:#  shifted, for no gain in coverage.  The width cell is therefore COVERED, not merely disclosed -- but
172:#  by that file, so an edit that deletes it re-opens this cell and this header says where to look.
173:#  `lcawidefold' (only s[3] of a 16-byte store reaches the port) REOPENS: that row IS the guest its
174:#  reopen condition names.
175:#  `len == 0' is refused and DISCLOSED (no producer); a DIRECTION swap (a store forwarded as a read, or the reverse)
176:#  *** WAS CALLED `a different class, caught by the value rows'.  IT IS A CELL OF THIS CLASS (the SWAP shape), and the
177:#  second half is HALF TRUE IN THE HALF THAT MATTERS: the value rows catch the MISSING READ -- RXW 143/185, and all 42
178:#  of its reds are read-VALUE rows with ZERO stub rows, so the access COUNT is still one -- and NOTHING WHATEVER
179:#  observes the ACQUIRED WRITE.  The proof is independent: put the read back and keep the write (RWall) and the
180:#  detector falls to 167/185, the stub and two tailr rows.  The sentence also conflated SWAP with ADD-OTHER, which are
181:#  different shapes. ***  *** THE CHANNEL AT LANE 3 IS AN EMULATOR TODO: dev_pckbc.c's PC-style
182:#  branch answers 0x61-0x6f (but 0x64) from `static int x; x++;', bit 0x10 its parity.  If that port is ever implemented
183:#  the stub rows go green whatever the access count -- which is why every stub session opens with a LIVENESS row (two
184:#  flat reads must flip bit 0x10) that reds when the oracle disappears. ***
185:#  *** HISTORY, because each step was a wrong record in its turn: repair 4 SCOPED this entry to lane 2 and lane 0 at the
186:#  direct widths and DISCLOSED lane 1 and lane-3 direct; its lane-3 warrant ("no read-side-effect port is present on
187:#  this probe's machine") was MEASURED FALSE by the Opus closing-pass-4 seat (L3gate, the stub at 0x63), its RD2
188:#  sentence was unscoped while its one row was (1,2) (R2w escaped at len 2/4/8, 111/111), and it gave the tail cells no
189:#  disposition at all (WT6/WTsplit 111/111) -- grok had corrected the lane-1 and wdc halves hours earlier.  Before that,
190:#  "the write arm's `return' removed" stood here UNSCOPED while its rows sat at (1,2) and (4,2) only (W4, 93/93). ***
191:#  *** TABLE 1'S CONTRACT CLAIMED MOST OF THIS AT 37 ROWS AND WAS MEASURED FALSE: the Opus
192:#  pass-2 seat built ten mutants that scored 37/37, nine changing a guest outcome -- no row stored at len
193:#  4/8 -- and measured that the split heads it DISCLOSED as needing a translated access split on K0SEG. ***
194:#  DISCLOSED, not caught: the len-0 refusal (no producer); the zero-fill of the positions other than
195:#  `ofs' (p_nozero -- EQUIVALENT: memory_rw's #95 pre-zero already clears the buffer); an address-decode
196:#  edit above port 0x3ff (p_port10 -- not an edit of the fix; measured 51/51 by the Opus closing seat.
197:#  This said "every probed port is below 0x400", and the split TAILS reach port 0x400 -- grok and the
198:#  Opus seat, closing pass 1 -- so the premise was wrong and the scoping reason is the one that stands);
199:#  the DISCARDED sparse byte offset a<4:0> -- the size field a<4:3> included (lcaisasize) -- which the
200:#  handler shifts away, so an edit keyed on it (the Opus closing-pass-3 seat's H1b, keyed on a<4:0> =
201:#  0x1c: the 4-byte split head, 93/93; this said "a<4:2> = 0x1c", impossible for a 3-bit slice -- agy,
202:#  closing pass 4) is not seen [widened from "the size field a<4:3>" by the flagship
203:#  ruling, closing pass 3]; the hardware QUADWORD ENCODING's route (lcaisaqword: on the
204:#  21066 a<6:5> = 11 with a<4:3> = 11 is a quadword at port P, which the model forwards to P|3 -- NOT the
205:#  len-8 lane this probe rows, which follows the same (P & 3) % len rule as every other width); the
206:#  missing PHYSICAL (lcaisaphys); the end-of-window clamp at port 0xffff (nothing is mapped there).
207:#  Disclosed BY CLASS (closing pass 2; each measured 77/77 by the Opus seat, regress/artifacts/lcaisalane/
208:#  opus_c2_20260914/): a decline or mis-decode keyed on an EXACT PORT VALUE (kimi-k3's `relative_addr ==
209:#  0x400 && len == 4' -- the p_port10 class: the body never tests the port against a constant); a
210:#  mis-decode keyed on the DATA (codex's `ofs == 2 && !data[2]', generally `data[ofs] ? data[ofs] :
211:#  data[0]' -- every VALUE store row carries a NONZERO correct byte and the `iir', `iirw' and `kbcq' rows
212:#  store all-zero words, so no row distinguishes a zero correct byte from a nonzero byte beside it [the
213:#  `iirw'/`kbcq' zero stores added to this list 2026-09-15 from closing pass 4 (codex, kimi CLI); a sentence
214:#  "'the correct byte is 0' is unrowed" stood here and was dropped as imprecise by the flagship ruling]).  [Corrected 2026-09-14 from closing pass 3 (codex, grok, kimi-k3): this said
215:#  "every store row carries a NONZERO correct byte", false since session 4h stores zero.]  EQUIVALENT on alphabook1, named: the inner forward's return value honoured on the
216:#  read arm, or forced to success on the write arm -- no ISA device there fails an access, and an
217:#  unmapped forward returns success with a zero fill.
218:#  EQUIVALENT by construction, named so no table is spent on them (kimi-k3, pass 2): restoring the dead
219:#  `% len' on the write arm's index (`ofs' is already reduced), and reinstating `if (ofs > len) exit(1)'
220:#  AFTER the fold (unsatisfiable once ofs < len) -- the reinstated exit that matters is on the UNFOLDED
221:#  lane, which the table's m_exit builds.
222:#
223:#  *** WHAT THE WITNESS (sessions 1-3) IS AND IS NOT.  IT IS A WITNESS, NOT A DETECTOR. ***
224:#
225:#  It establishes, on unmodified `alphabook1' with no disk image and no OS: that the sparse window
226:#  IS live and does reach ISA devices (`live', 0x55 from the 8042 through the same handler); that a
227:#  BYTE read of a device register whose port is 1 mod 4 completes successfully and hands the guest
228:#  zero while the device's own byte is demonstrably present (`ofs1 devsig' + `ofs1 wide'); and that
229:#  a byte read of a register whose port is 2 or 3 mod 4 ends the host with the arm's own diagnostic.
230:#
231:#  It is NOT a detector of any repair.  `alive' greens equally for a decline, for a latched
232:#  announce, for a fault and for a full implementation, and `ofs1 zero' greens for any fix that
233:#  hands the byte back.  A fix round owes rows that discriminate those -- at minimum, per the codex
234:#  design reading (regress/artifacts/lcaisalane/codex_design_20260910.err): a REFUSED access must
235:#  leave the destination register holding its previous value (the `answer' channel this probe
236:#  already reads), the refusal must repeat without re-announcing, and a supported lane must still
237:#  deliver the correctly packed byte at every width.
238:#
239:#  ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
240:#  SHAPE, and every element of it is answering a specific objection:
241:#
242:#    1.  ONE PROCESS PER OUTCOME, CONTROL FIRST.  A death ends the process, so the two killing
243:#        lanes get their own runs -- but inside each run the DEVICE SIGNATURE and the WIDE control
244:#        are taken BEFORE the killing access, so between the access that lives and the access that
245:#        dies only the WIDTH differs: same address, same window, same process, same instant.
246:#    2.  ZERO READBACK ALONE PROVES NOTHING, and the design seat said so in those words.  Unmapped
247:#        space, a missed device and a zero-filled failure buffer all read 0 (`memory_rw.c''s #95
248:#        pre-zero).  So `ofs1 zero' is never asserted on its own: `ofs1 devsig' reads the SAME
249:#        register through the flat ISA mapping and gets a nonzero value the device alone produces,
250:#        and `ofs1 wide' reads it through THIS handler at len 4 and finds the byte sitting in lane
251:#        1 -- so the byte reaches the packing loop and the loop is what drops it.
252:#    3.  THE DESTINATION REGISTER IS PRE-SEEDED WITH A SENTINEL before every load.  A load that
253:#        FAULTS leaves the register untouched (cpu_alpha_instr_loadstore.c returns before
254:#        writeback when memory_rw fails), so `ofs1 answer' distinguishes "the model answered zero"
255:#        from "the access failed and the guest kept its old value".  On HEAD it is the former: a
256:#        FALSE SUCCESS.  This is the discriminator the design seat asked for, taken pre-fix.
257:#    4.  ATTRIBUTION IS BY THE ARM'S OWN TEXT, NEVER BY EXIT STATUS.  `in lca_isa access function'
258:#        appears exactly once in src/.  A death for any other reason -- a bad encoding, a different
259:#        arm -- reds the attrib row instead of passing as a reproduction.
260:#    5.  EVERY ACCESS IS A GUEST INSTRUCTION.  `lca_isa' is registered DM_DEFAULT, so debugger
261:#        `dump' would skip the handler entirely and read the pre-zero.  Every `put d' below targets
262:#        SCRATCH, which is RAM.
263:#    6.  STAY ON `step'.  Nothing here needs a host timer: the 8042 reply and the UART register
264:#        file are synchronous with the guest access.
265:#
266:#  THE NEGATIVE CONTROL IS ISA PORT 0x300 (`ctl'), which is 0 mod 4 and has NO device behind it.
267:#  It survives and answers zero.  That is what attributes the two deaths to the LANE rather than to
268:#  the sparse window, to the byte width, to K0SEG, or to hitting empty ISA space -- and note the
269:#  two killing ports DO have a device behind them, so the pair brackets the question from both
270:#  sides.
271:#
272:#  Usage:  lca_isalane_probe.py <gxemul-binary> [label]
273:#          LCAISALANE_RAW=<dir>   -- optional: dump each session's raw pty transcript there
276:BIN   = sys.argv[1]
277:LABEL = sys.argv[2] if len(sys.argv) > 2 else "?"
278:MACH  = "alphabook1"          #  device_add(machine, "lca") is ST_ALPHABOOK1 ONLY
279:RAW   = os.environ.get("LCAISALANE_RAW", "")
281:rows = []
282:def row(name, got, want, note=""):
288:# ---------------------------------------------------------------- encoder (memory format only)
289:ZERO, T0, T1, T2, T3, T4, T5, T6 = 31, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7
290:#  T6 is the highest used.  A probe reaching for an undefined register name dies with a NameError
291:#  rather than emitting a bad encoding, which is the safe direction (#516 found this the hard way).
293:def mem(op, ra, rb, disp):
297:def LDA (ra, disp, rb=ZERO): return mem(0x08, ra, rb, disp)
298:def LDAH(ra, disp, rb=ZERO): return mem(0x09, ra, rb, disp)
299:def LDBU(ra, disp, rb):      return mem(0x0a, ra, rb, disp)   # BWX byte load  -> memory_rw len 1
300:def LDL (ra, disp, rb):      return mem(0x28, ra, rb, disp)   # longword load  -> memory_rw len 4
301:def LDQ (ra, disp, rb):      return mem(0x29, ra, rb, disp)   # quadword load  -> memory_rw len 8
302:def STB (ra, disp, rb):      return mem(0x0e, ra, rb, disp)   # BWX byte store -> memory_rw len 1
303:def LDWU(ra, disp, rb):      return mem(0x0c, ra, rb, disp)   # BWX word load  -> memory_rw len 2  (#530)
304:def STW (ra, disp, rb):      return mem(0x0d, ra, rb, disp)   # BWX word store -> memory_rw len 2  (#530)
305:def STL (ra, disp, rb):      return mem(0x2c, ra, rb, disp)   # longword store -> memory_rw len 4  (#530 pass 2)
306:def STQ (ra, disp, rb):      return mem(0x2d, ra, rb, disp)   # quadword store -> memory_rw len 8  (#530 pass 2)
307:#  LDBU/STB are opcodes 0x0a/0x0e and are decoded with `loadstore_type = 0' in
308:#  cpu_alpha_instr.c, which selects the 1-byte template in cpu_alpha_instr_loadstore.c -- so the
309:#  handler under test really does see len == 1.  Not a new capability: lca_irqstub_probe.py already
310:#  drives STB on this machine, and there is no BWX gate in this tree.
312:def load_const(reg, val):
324:SENT = 0x0bad1d8a         #  the pre-seed.  A load that FAULTS leaves this in the register.
325:#  #530: was 0x0bad1dea, whose low byte 0xea has bits 5-6 SET -- so `ofs1 zero', masked to
326:#  LSR_FIXED (0x60), read a declined load (sentinel kept) as the LSR byte and went GREEN under a
327:#  decline (measured on the F2 A variant, regress/artifacts/lcaisalane/opus_f2_20260913/).  0x8a
328:#  has bits 5-6 CLEAR.  Re-pinned FIRST, HEAD re-run 13/16 with the same three reds, THEN the fix
329:#  (the oracle-correction order; FABLE_F2_RULING_20260914.md R3).
330:def sent(reg): return load_const(reg, SENT)
332:# ---------------------------------------------------------------- addresses
333:K0SEG        = 0xfffffc0000000000
334:def k0(p):   return K0SEG | p
336:LCA_PCI_SIO  = 0x1c0000000               # alpha_lcareg.h; DEVINIT maps 0x10000<<5 bytes here as
338:LCA_ISA_BASE = LCA_PCI_SIO + 0x10000000  # dev_lca.c:47.  bus_isa_init put the real devices here,
340:def SP(port):   return LCA_PCI_SIO  + (port << 5)   # the guest-visible sparse address
341:def FLAT(port): return LCA_ISA_BASE + port          # the internal flat mapping
343:#  ---- the ports, and why each one ----------------------------------------------------------
344:KBC_CMD, KBC_DAT = 0x64, 0x60      # 8042: both 0 mod 4.  0xAA is the standard self-test command
345:KBC_SELFTEST, KBC_REPLY = 0xAA, 0x55
346:UART_LSR   = 0x3fd                 # 1 mod 4 -> ofs 1.  DEVICE_ACCESS(ns16550) sets
347:LSR_FIXED  = 0x60                  #   `d->reg[com_lsr] |= LSR_TXRDY | LSR_TSRE' at the TOP of
352:UART_MSR   = 0x3fe                 # 2 mod 4 -> ofs 2.  Same handler unconditionally sets
353:MSR_FIXED  = 0xb0                  #   MSR_DCD|MSR_DSR|MSR_CTS = 0x80|0x20|0x10, and nothing else
355:UART_SCR   = 0x3ff                 # 3 mod 4 -> ofs 3.  reg 7 falls to the ns16550 `default:' arm,
356:SCR_VALUE  = 0x5a                  #   which stores and returns the byte -- a plain scratchpad.
357:NODEV_PORT = 0x300                 # 0 mod 4, and bus_isa_init registers nothing at 0x300.
359:SCRATCH      = 0x01020000
360:CODE         = 0x01040000
363:DUMMY = os.path.join(os.path.dirname(os.path.abspath(__file__)), "alpha_dummy.bin")
364:if not os.path.exists(DUMMY):
368:# ---------------------------------------------------------------- session
369:def session(cmds, settle, spin):
409:def printed(t, reg):
413:#  #530 repair 6: `printed' is re.search -- it returns the FIRST print of a register and nothing else.  That is
414:#  correct for every session written before repair 6, because each prints a given register once.  The 8253 sessions
415:#  print T3/T4/T5 once per PHASE, ~20 phases per session, so reading them with `printed' would silently hand every
416:#  row PHASE 1's value -- a whole session vacuous by construction, and GREEN.  `nth' is the positional form (the
417:#  idiom crime_width_probe.py already uses).  `printed' is deliberately NOT reimplemented in terms of it, so the
418:#  existing 185 rows cannot move.
419:def nth(t, reg, n):
424:def n_prints(t, reg):
428:def build(consts, phases):
447:def run(tag, consts, phases, want_regs, settle=3.0, spin=4.0):
473:def evidence(txt, tag):
479:def hx(v):
482:ATTRIB = "in lca_isa access function"     # unique in src/; the arm's own diagnostic
489:# ================================================================ SESSION 1: the surviving rows
490:#  [A] STB 0xAA -> sparse(0x64)      8042 command, ofs 0
491:#  [B] LDBU     <- sparse(0x60)      8042 data,    ofs 0   -> 0x55            `live'
492:#  [C] LDBU     <- flat(0x3fd)       LSR via the internal mapping             `ofs1 devsig'
493:#  [D] LDBU     <- sparse(0x3fd)     LSR, len 1, ofs 1 -> 0 on HEAD           `ofs1 zero'
494:#  [E] LDL      <- sparse(0x3fd)     LSR, len 4, ofs 1 -> byte in lane 1      `ofs1 wide'
495:#  [F] LDBU     <- sparse(0x300)     no device, ofs 0                         `ctl'
496:C1 = [k0(SP(KBC_CMD)), KBC_SELFTEST, k0(SP(KBC_DAT)), k0(FLAT(UART_LSR)),
498:P1 = [([LDQ(T1, 0, T0), LDQ(T2, 8, T0), STB(T2, 0, T1),
509:row("base alive", "alive" if a1 else "dead", "alive",
512:#  ---- LIVENESS + DEVICE SIGNATURE, through the handler under test ---------------------------
513:#  Only the 8042 answers 0x55 to a load from ISA 0x60 after 0xAA reached ISA 0x64.  RAM and
514:#  unmapped space cannot, and this proves the SPARSE window reaches devices at all.
515:row("live", hx(b_live), hx(KBC_REPLY),
518:#  ---- the device byte exists, taken through the OTHER mapping --------------------------------
519:row("ofs1 devsig", hx(None if b_flat is None else b_flat & LSR_FIXED), hx(LSR_FIXED),
522:#  ---- THE DEFECT ROW -------------------------------------------------------------------------
523:#  ORACLE POLARITY is the witness ladder's: assert the CORRECT behaviour, so this is RED pre-fix
524:#  and GREEN post-fix with the SAME script.  WANT = THE DEVICE BYTE.  For len == 1 the caller's
525:#  buffer has exactly one lane and it is data[0]; `i == ofs' with ofs 1 selects nothing, so the
526:#  byte is dropped.  (A DECLINE is the other defensible disposition and would also red this row --
527:#  which is correct: a decline must not be reported as a successful read of zero.  What a fix may
528:#  NOT do is keep answering a false zero.)
529:row("ofs1 zero", hx(None if b_byte is None else b_byte & LSR_FIXED),
533:#  ---- the zero is an ANSWER, not a fault -----------------------------------------------------
534:#  A load whose memory_rw fails returns before writeback (cpu_alpha_instr_loadstore.c), leaving
535:#  the sentinel.  This row says which of the two happened, and on HEAD it is a FALSE SUCCESS.
536:row("ofs1 answer", "completed" if (b_byte is not None and b_byte != SENT) else "faulted/absent",
540:#  ---- the byte DOES reach the packing loop ---------------------------------------------------
541:#  Same address, same process, len 4 instead of 1: ofs 1 <= 4, so the loop writes data[1] and the
542:#  guest sees the byte shifted into lane 1.  This is what makes `ofs1 zero' a lane-packing finding
543:#  rather than "the sparse window does not reach the UART".
544:row("ofs1 wide", hx(None if b_wide is None else (b_wide >> 8) & LSR_FIXED), hx(LSR_FIXED),
547:#  ---- the negative control for the window ----------------------------------------------------
548:row("ctl", "alive" if a1 else "dead", "alive",
550:row("ctl zero", hx(b_ctl), hx(0),
555:# ================================================================ SESSION 2: ofs 2 ends the host
556:#  [A] LDBU <- flat(0x3fe)     MSR through the internal mapping   -> 0xb0
557:#  [B] LDL  <- sparse(0x3fe)   MSR, len 4, ofs 2 -> byte in lane 2, SURVIVES
558:#  [C] LDBU <- sparse(0x3fe)   MSR, len 1, ofs 2 -> 2 > 1 -> exit(1)
559:C2 = [k0(FLAT(UART_MSR)), k0(SP(UART_MSR))]
560:P2 = [([LDQ(T1, 0, T0)] + sent(T2) + [LDBU(T2, 0, T1)], "t2"),
567:row("ofs2 devsig", hx(m_flat), hx(MSR_FIXED),
569:row("ofs2 wide", hx(None if m_wide is None else (m_wide >> 16) & 0xff), hx(MSR_FIXED),
571:row("ofs2 exit", "alive" if a2 else "dead", "alive",
573:row("ofs2 attrib", "yes" if (a2 or ATTRIB in t2x) else "died-elsewhere", "yes",
578:# ================================================================ SESSION 3: ofs 3 ends the host
579:#  [A] STB 0x5a -> flat(0x3ff);  LDBU <- flat(0x3ff)   scratch register, through the flat mapping
580:#  [B] LDL  <- sparse(0x3ff)     len 4, ofs 3 -> byte in lane 3, SURVIVES
581:#  [C] LDBU <- sparse(0x3ff)     len 1, ofs 3 -> 3 > 1 -> exit(1)
582:C3 = [k0(FLAT(UART_SCR)), SCR_VALUE, k0(SP(UART_SCR))]
583:P3 = [([LDQ(T1, 0, T0), LDQ(T2, 8, T0), STB(T2, 0, T1)] + sent(T3) + [LDBU(T3, 0, T1)], "t3"),
590:row("ofs3 devsig", hx(s_flat), hx(SCR_VALUE),
592:row("ofs3 wide", hx(None if s_wide is None else (s_wide >> 24) & 0xff), hx(SCR_VALUE),
594:row("ofs3 exit", "alive" if a3 else "dead", "alive",
596:row("ofs3 attrib", "yes" if (a3 or ATTRIB in t3x) else "died-elsewhere", "yes",
601:# ================================================================ #530: THE DETECTOR SESSIONS
602:#  Everything above is the rung-3 WITNESS, unchanged except for the sentinel.  Everything below is
603:#  the DETECTOR for the #530 fix (disposition B, FABLE_F2_RULING_20260914.md): the byte for port P
604:#  sits at buffer position `(P & 3) % len' on BOTH arms -- the rule HEAD already ran at every served
605:#  cell -- so no access ends the host and none is answered with a discarded byte.  Each row names
606:#  the enumerated mutant it kills (R3's table) and the channel: a FULL-REGISTER read (so a declined
607:#  load, which keeps the sentinel, and a replicated or mis-laned byte all differ from the want), or
608:#  a store read back through the FLAT mapping, which is a plain forward independent of the handler.
609:#
610:#  Ports: LSR 0x3fd (1 mod 4; TXRDY|TSRE forced), MSR 0x3fe (2 mod 4; reads reg|0xb0), IER 0x3f9
611:#  (1 mod 4; stores and returns the byte), SCR 0x3ff (3 mod 4; scratch).
612:UART_IER = 0x3f9
613:UART_MCR = 0x3fc              # 0 mod 4: modem control, stores and returns its low bits (pass 2)
614:DRETRIED = []
616:def prints_in_order(txt):
621:def dsession(tag, consts, phases, names, settle=3.0, spin=4.0):
641:#  ---- SESSION 4: the cells that never ended the host (no mutant in the table can kill them) --
642:#  slots: 0 SP(LSR) 1 FLAT(LSR) 2 SP(MSR) 3 FLAT(MSR) 4 SP(IER) 5 FLAT(IER)
643:C4 = [k0(SP(UART_LSR)), k0(FLAT(UART_LSR)), k0(SP(UART_MSR)), k0(FLAT(UART_MSR)),
645:P4 = [([LDQ(T1, 8, T0)]  + sent(T2) + [LDBU(T2, 0, T1)], "t2"),                 # lsr  (flat)
659:row("fold alive", "alive" if a4 else "dead", "alive",
