952:#  ---- `kbcmd' (#530 repair 6): THE SAME 8042, A DIFFERENT ORACLE -- and the difference is the whole point. ------
953:#  `kbcq' above asks "does the queued reply SURVIVE the store?".  That is a LATCH question: it separates zero accesses
954:#  from at least one, and a DOUBLED store cannot move it, because two stores leave the reply just as queued as one.
955:#  `kbcmd' asks "did the command produce EXACTLY ONE reply?", which is a COUNT question and is what this class needs:
956:#      DIB=1 (a reply is queued)  ->  pop 0x55  ->  DIB=0 (the queue is now EMPTY).
957:#  A doubled store delivers 0xAA twice, so TWO replies queue and DIB is STILL 1 after the single pop.  A phantom read
958:#  (x_wfallthru) pops the reply early, so DIB is already 0 BEFORE the pop.  The two escapes move OPPOSITE rows.
959:#  The command goes through lca_isa at SP(0x64): 0x64 & 3 == 0, so ofs = 0 % len = 0 at every width, and a const of
960:#  0xAA puts the command byte at position 0 for all four.  One session per width -- the 8042 queue is FIFO and
961:#  stateful, which is why `kbcq' is built the same way.  No new device: this is the 8042 the tail rows already use.
