1196:#  ================================================================================================================
1197:#  #530 REPAIR 6 -- THE 8253 SESSIONS.  The forward-COUNT class's channel at lanes 0/1/2 and the split tails.
1198:#
1199:#  *** WHY THE CHANNEL CHANGED, AND IT IS THE FINDING OF CLOSING PASS 5.  Repair 5 put CMOS REG C at lane 1 and
1200:#  ns16550 IIR at lane 2.  `dev_mc146818.c:565' -- the read does `d->reg[MC_REGC*4] = 0x00;', so a second read finds
1201:#  it already zero; the write path is a plain assignment.  `dev_ns16550.c:211-213' -- the read clears IIR_TXRDY once;
1202:#  the write path is `d->fcr = idata;'.  BOTH ARE ONE-SHOT LATCHES, AND A LATCH SEPARATES *ZERO* ACCESSES FROM
1203:#  *AT LEAST ONE*.  IT CAN NEVER SEPARATE *ONE* FROM *TWO*, which is exactly and only what a forward-COUNT class
1204:#  asks.  Measured: WWall 169/185 is sixteen stub rows and NOTHING else; RWall 167/185 is 16 stub + 2 tailr;
1205:#  RD2post 161/185 has no architectural carrier at lanes 1-2.  The emulator-TODO stub was carrying those cells alone
1206:#  because THE STUB IS AN ALTERNATOR AND ITS NEIGHBOURS WERE LATCHES. ***
1207:#
1208:#      THE RULE, checkable by reading: A FORWARD-COUNT ROW NEEDS A COUNTER OR AN ALTERNATOR, NEVER A LATCH.
1209:#
1210:#  *** THE ROW TAXONOMY THAT FOLLOWS FROM IT, because conflating the two kinds is what produced three passes of
1211:#  escapes.  A row here is one of TWO things and the contract must say which: ***
1212:#      COUNT rows -- they answer "HOW MANY forwards reached the port?"  Their channel is a COUNTER or an ALTERNATOR:
1213:#        the 8253 flip-flops (`pit0/1/2', `pitT'), the 8042 reply queue used as `kbcmd' (did the command produce
1214:#        EXACTLY ONE reply?), and the pckbc stub's access parity (`stub0'-`stub3').
1215:#      VALUE / POSITION rows -- they answer "WHICH BYTE landed WHERE?"  Their channel is a one-shot LATCH or a stored
1216:#        value: `cmos' (REG C: the read clears it), `iir'/`iirw'/`iirr' (the read clears TXRDY once), the
1217:#        store/read-back rows, `ofs*', `h*', `tailr'.  THESE ARE GOOD ROWS AND THEY STAY -- they carry the POSITION
1218:#        rule, which is what #530 actually fixed.  What they CANNOT do is separate ONE forward from TWO, so no
1219:#        forward-count cell may rest on them, and before repair 6 three cells did.
1220:#
1221:#  THE MECHANISM, read from source and not from the brief (`src/devices/dev_8253.c'):
1222:#    * ports 0x40/0x41/0x42 are `relative_addr' 0/1/2 = LANES 0/1/2; port 0x43 is the mode register = lane 3.
1223:#    * the flip-flop advance sits INSIDE the counter case and fires on EVERY counter access, read and write, while
1224:#      (mode_byte & 0x30) == I8253_TIMER_16BIT.  `wr_msb' toggles on writes, `rd_msb' on reads -- SEPARATE PER
1225:#      DIRECTION, which is why a read-parity row is blind to an added WRITE and the PW shape exists.
1226:#    * a control word REWINDS both flip-flops for that counter, so every phase is self-starting.  EVERY PHASE MUST
1227:#      OPEN WITH ONE, or phases leak parity into each other.
1228:#
1229:#  *** LANE 3 IS UNREACHABLE ON THIS CHANNEL, AND THAT IS MEASURED, NOT REASONED: a read of the mode port returns
1230:#  `mode_byte' with no side effect and advances no flip-flop, and a repeated identical control word is idempotent.
1231:#  `cdxZL3', whose entire edit is at lane 3, scores 150/150 against these rows.  Lane 3 therefore KEEPS `stub3' and
1232:#  its mandatory liveness row -- which RB1 already licenses -- and this contract says so rather than implying the
1233:#  8253 covers everything. ***
1234:#
1235:#  COST, measured by the prototype rather than estimated: a 16-bit MSB write to counters 1 and 2 emits an UNLATCHED
1236:#  `fatal()' (`dev_8253.c:198' `TODO: DMA refresh?' and `:200' `TODO: 8253 tone generation?'), 18 of each per session.
1237:#  It moves no row -- the probe parses `print' output -- and it is filed separately as `pit8253flood'.
1238:#  An MSB write to counter 0 instead calls `timer_add'/`timer_update_frequency' with hz = 1193182/counter, so the
1239:#  programmed counter is kept LARGE (~250 Hz); a small one would install a high-frequency host timer.
1240:#  ================================================================================================================
